<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Chronic low back and pelvic pain after remote spinal and pelvic fractures and spinal surgery, with neuropathic features in the right leg; linked to the documented 1843 injuries: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the right ankle and foot after multiple leg fractures and a foot dislocation: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Right leg weakness, atrophy, and shortening after childhood poliomyelitis; a congenital spinal anomaly has been proposed retrospectively: active, provisional [S]</item><item>Long-term opioid therapy for chronic pain, 60 MME a day at intake: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Vitamin D deficiency: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Physical medicine and rehabilitation referral for a multimodal rehabilitation plan: exercise therapy, bracing and orthotic review, a shoe lift for the leg-length difference, and function goals. Guideline: Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Low Back Pain (2022) [S]</item><item>Nonopioid treatment first: duloxetine titrated to 60 mg daily, scheduled acetaminophen up to 3 g a day, and topical diclofenac for the ankle. Guideline: CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, osteoarthritis and depression monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Opioid risk mitigation: review benefits and harms together, check the prescription drug monitoring program, urine drug test with consent, prescribe naloxone 4 mg nasal spray and teach a family member, and avoid benzodiazepines and other sedatives. Guideline: CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022) [S]</item><item>Collaborative, gradual opioid taper of about 10% of the dose a month, paused if pain or function worsens; never stop opioids abruptly. Guideline: CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022) [S]</item><item>Measure pain and function with the PEG at every visit and depressive symptoms with the PHQ-9 every two to four weeks; complete a structured safety assessment if the PHQ-9 reaches 10 or item 9 is positive. Guideline: CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, osteoarthritis and depression monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Check ALT periodically on regular acetaminophen, and replete vitamin D. Guideline: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, osteoarthritis and depression monographs (2026) [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>
